AFS-002 — Canonical Atlas Resource

Document ID: AFS-002
Title: Canonical Atlas Resource
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Atlas Resource (CAR), the universal foundational abstraction of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Atlas Resource establishes the common identity, structure, lifecycle, governance, and interoperability characteristics shared by every identifiable entity within the Atlas ecosystem.

Every Atlas specification SHALL derive its domain-specific resources from the Canonical Atlas Resource.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Canonical Atlas Resource
Universal inheritance model
Resource identity
Resource characteristics
Resource lifecycle
Resource relationships
Resource governance
Resource extensibility

This specification applies to every identifiable Atlas entity.

3. Canonical Definition

A Canonical Atlas Resource (CAR) is the universal foundational entity representing any identifiable, governable, versionable, referenceable, and interoperable artifact within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework.

A Canonical Atlas Resource provides shared capabilities.

It SHALL NOT define domain-specific behavior.

4. Objectives

The Canonical Atlas Resource SHALL:

Establish a universal root type.
Eliminate duplication across specifications.
Provide consistent identity.
Support governance.
Enable interoperability.
Support extensibility.
Preserve traceability.
Enable long-term evolution.
5. Universal Resource Principle

Atlas adopts the following foundational principle.

Every identifiable entity within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework SHALL be represented as a Canonical Atlas Resource.

This principle applies uniformly across all Atlas specification families.

6. Resource Principles

Every Canonical Atlas Resource SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Identity

Every resource SHALL possess a canonical identity.

Uniqueness

Every resource SHALL be uniquely identifiable.

Persistence

Resource identity SHALL remain persistent throughout its lifecycle.

Governance

Every resource SHALL be governable.

Versionability

Every resource SHALL support governed evolution where applicable.

Traceability

Every resource SHALL remain traceable throughout its existence.

Interoperability

Every resource SHALL support standardized interaction.

Extensibility

Resources SHALL permit governed extension without altering canonical semantics.

7. Canonical Resource Characteristics

Every Canonical Atlas Resource SHALL possess the following characteristics.

Characteristic	Description
Identity	Canonical unique identity
Type	Resource classification
Namespace	Logical ownership
URI	Canonical Atlas URI
Metadata	Shared descriptive information
Lifecycle	Resource lifecycle state
Version	Version information
Relationships	Connections to other resources
References	Canonical references
Governance	Applicable governance
Security	Technical security metadata
Capabilities	Declared capabilities
Extensions	Approved extension points

These characteristics form the minimum contract of every Atlas Resource.

8. Universal Inheritance Model

Every Atlas specification SHALL derive its primary resource types from the Canonical Atlas Resource.

Canonical Atlas Resource (CAR)
│
├── Canonical Atlas Object (ADS)
├── Canonical Policy Object (APS)
├── Canonical Workflow Object (AWS)
├── Canonical Cognitive Object (AIS)
│
├── Canonical Event
├── Canonical Document
├── Canonical Package
├── Canonical Organization
├── Canonical Person
├── Canonical Agent
├── Canonical Digital Twin
├── Canonical Asset
├── Canonical Sensor
├── Canonical Service
└── Future Canonical Resources

Derived resource types SHALL preserve the semantics of the Canonical Atlas Resource.

9. Resource Relationships

Canonical Atlas Resources MAY relate to one another through governed relationship types.

Examples include:

Contains
References
Depends On
Implements
Governs
Executes
Produces
Consumes
Represents
Owns

Relationship semantics SHALL be defined by the applicable specification family.

10. Resource Lifecycle

Every Canonical Atlas Resource SHALL support the following conceptual lifecycle.

Defined
   │
Created
   │
Active
   │
Modified
   │
Deprecated
   │
Archived

Specialized resource types MAY extend this lifecycle while preserving its canonical meaning.

11. Resource Governance

Every Canonical Atlas Resource SHALL define:

Resource Owner
Governing Authority
Applicable Policies
Applicable Standards
Audit Requirements
Retention Requirements

Governance SHALL remain independently auditable.

12. Resource Extensibility

Canonical Atlas Resources SHALL support governed extension.

Extensions:

SHALL preserve canonical semantics.
SHALL remain namespace-qualified.
SHALL declare compatibility.
SHALL remain independently versioned.
13. Resource Quality

Every Canonical Atlas Resource SHALL satisfy:

Correctness
Consistency
Traceability
Interoperability
Extensibility
Explainability
Auditability
Governance Compliance

Quality SHALL be independently verifiable.

14. Conformance

A Canonical Atlas Resource implementation conforms if it:

Implements the mandatory resource characteristics.
Preserves canonical identity.
Supports governed lifecycle management.
Supports standardized relationships.
Maintains interoperability.
Supports extension.
Preserves traceability.
15. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Semantic resource discovery.
Autonomous resource management.
Federated resource registries.
Digital resource twins.
AI-native resource capabilities.
Machine-verifiable resource semantics.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical resource semantics.

